                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 1240,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1056,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Picks a list of positions along one dimension - rows 0, 3 and 7, say - in the order given, and keeps the dimension.\n\nUse it to pull chosen joints out of a joint tensor, to reorder channels, to repeat a row, or to reverse an axis with a descending list. The indices can come from anywhere in the patch: a message, t.argsort, a t.argwhere result.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.index_select <dim: int>\nWith no argument the node works along dimension 0 (rows) and shows no\ndim inlet. Give a dim (t.index_select 1) to get a dim box you can change.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.index_select 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the selection on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\nindices:\nThe positions to take, as whole numbers: a list (0 3 7), an array or a tensor. They are converted to integers. Repeats are allowed (2 2 5) and the order is kept. A 2-D list of indices is flattened to 1-D first. A position past the end prints an error in the console and nothing is sent; negative positions are refused the same way (unlike t[]). The node sends nothing until indices have arrived. This inlet is cold: changing the list does not send - the next tensor uses it. In the demo a metro re-sends the source ten times a second.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet - only when an argument was given):\nWhich dimension the indices refer to: 0 rows, 1 columns. A dim the tensor does not have sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe selected rows (or columns), in the order of the indices. Same number of dimensions as the input; along dim it is as long as the index list. It is a new tensor, not a view.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the numbered source - tens digit the row, units digit the column. t.index_select 1 takes columns. Click a list: 0 3 7 gives three columns, 3, 13 ... 93 down the middle one; 9 8 7 ... 0 reverses the columns (the units digits now count down); 2 2 5 5 repeats two of them. Set dim to 0 and the same lists pick rows instead.\n\nRELATED:\nt.select takes ONE position and removes the dimension. t[] slices by a typed range. t.take treats the tensor as flat. t.take_along_dim takes a different position in each row. t.argsort makes index lists that sort. np.[] takes lists of positions in numpy.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1040,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Picks a list of positions along one dimension - rows 0, 3 and 7, say - in the order given, and keeps the dimension.\n\nUse it to pull chosen joints out of a joint tensor, to reorder channels, to repeat a row, or to reverse an axis with a descending list. The indices can come from anywhere in the patch: a message, t.argsort, a t.argwhere result.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.index_select <dim: int>\nWith no argument the node works along dimension 0 (rows) and shows no\ndim inlet. Give a dim (t.index_select 1) to get a dim box you can change.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.index_select 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array is converted to a float32 tensor). Sends the selection on arrival. This is the only inlet that sends.\n\nindices:\nThe positions to take, as whole numbers: a list (0 3 7), an array or a tensor. They are converted to integers. Repeats are allowed (2 2 5) and the order is kept. A 2-D list of indices is flattened to 1-D first. A position past the end prints an error in the console and nothing is sent; negative positions are refused the same way (unlike t[]). The node sends nothing until indices have arrived. This inlet is cold: changing the list does not send - the next tensor uses it. In the demo a metro re-sends the source ten times a second.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet - only when an argument was given):\nWhich dimension the indices refer to: 0 rows, 1 columns. A dim the tensor does not have sends nothing.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe selected rows (or columns), in the order of the indices. Same number of dimensions as the input; along dim it is as long as the index list. It is a new tensor, not a view.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe left heat_map is the numbered source - tens digit the row, units digit the column. t.index_select 1 takes columns. Click a list: 0 3 7 gives three columns, 3, 13 ... 93 down the middle one; 9 8 7 ... 0 reverses the columns (the units digits now count down); 2 2 5 5 repeats two of them. Set dim to 0 and the same lists pick rows instead.\n\nRELATED:\nt.select takes ONE position and removes the dimension. t[] slices by a typed range. t.take treats the tensor as flat. t.take_along_dim takes a different position in each row. t.argsort makes index lists that sort. np.[] takes lists of positions in numpy."
                },
